course: physics
lesson: 44
title: Olympiad: speed, space and seasons ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: The last olympiad lesson goes fastest and furthest: speeds, rockets, the spinning Earth, the Moon, tides and seasons, in puzzles where one hidden step makes all the difference.
explain: Speed puzzles always need two numbers, a distance and a time; to compare two runners, work out how long each would take over the same distance.
explain: Some sky puzzles hide a famous myth: the seasons are not caused by the Earth coming nearer to the Sun, because we are nearest in January, in the middle of the northern winter.
explain: Picture Jack in Sydney on Christmas Day: "Stinkin' hot arvo, mate, off to the beach in me togs": "arvo" means afternoon and "togs" means a swimsuit, and it is summer there because the southern half of the Earth leans towards the Sun in December.
explain: Some claims sound like wild hyperbole but are plain fact, such as "we're whizzing round the Sun at 100,000 kilometres an hour right now", while others that sound sensible, such as "the Moon has a dark side that never gets sunshine", are myths.
fact: A car going steadily at 60 kilometres an hour covers 1 kilometre each minute, so 20 kilometres in 20 minutes.
fact: To compare runners who ran different distances, work out the time each would take for the same distance: 200 metres in 32 seconds is 100 metres in 16 seconds.
fact: At twice the speed a car needs about four times the braking distance, so at half the speed it needs only about a quarter.
fact: In the morning the Sun is in the east, so shadows point west; at midday in Britain the Sun is in the south, so shadows point north; in the evening they point east.
fact: Most beaches have two high tides a day, about 12 and a half hours apart, and each day's tides come about 50 minutes later than the day before.
fact: Every fourth year is a leap year with an extra day, 29 February; 2020, 2024 and 2028 are leap years.
fact: The Earth is nearest to the Sun in early January; the seasons come from the tilt of the Earth, not from its distance.
fact: The Moon is always half lit by the Sun, and at new Moon the side we never see is in full sunshine.
fact: The Moon spins once for each trip round the Earth, so we always see the same face.
fact: A rocket throws hot gas out behind it and the gas pushes the rocket forward, so it does not need air to push on.
fact: In a braking car you lurch forward, but a helium balloon on a string leans backwards, because the heavier air sloshes forward and pushes it back.
fact: The Earth carries us round the Sun at about 100,000 kilometres an hour, and the ground at the equator spins at about 1,600 kilometres an hour, but the motion is so smooth that we feel nothing.
fact: From slowest to fastest: a snail, the fastest human runners at about 40 kilometres an hour, a cheetah at about 100, a passenger plane at about 900, the space station at about 28,000.
exercise: A car goes steadily at 60 kilometres an hour. How far does it go in 20 minutes? | 20 kilometres | 60 kilometres | 3 kilometres | 120 kilometres
exercise: Ana runs 100 metres in 15 seconds. Ben runs 200 metres in 32 seconds at an even pace. Who is faster? | Ana | Ben, because he ran further | They are exactly as fast | You cannot tell without their ages
exercise: A car at 40 kilometres an hour needs about 10 metres to brake. About how far does it need at 20 kilometres an hour? | About 2 and a half metres | About 5 metres, half as far | About 10 metres | About 20 metres
exercise: Put them in order from slowest to fastest: a passenger plane, a cheetah, the fastest human runner, a snail. | Snail, runner, cheetah, plane | Snail, cheetah, runner, plane | Runner, snail, cheetah, plane | Snail, runner, plane, cheetah
exercise: Early in the morning, which way does your shadow point? | West | To the east | North, always | Straight down under your feet
exercise: High tide on a beach is at 9 in the morning. About when is the next high tide? | About half past 9 that evening | About 9 the next morning | About 3 that afternoon | Exactly at 9 that evening
exercise: Morning high tide is at 8:00 on Monday. About when is the morning high tide on Tuesday? | About 8:50 | Exactly 8:00 | About 7:10 | About 20:00
exercise: Which of these years has a 29 February? | 2028 | 2026 | 2027 | 2030
exercise: Zoe was born on 29 February 2016. How many real 29 February birthdays has she had by the one in 2028? | 3 | 12 | 4 | 2
exercise: When is the Earth nearest to the Sun? | In early January | In midsummer, in July | In June, on the longest day | Never, it is always just as far
exercise: Why is the far side of the Moon NOT always dark? | At new Moon it is in full sunshine | Its rocks glow brightly on their own | The Earth lights it up from behind it | Stars shine on it all through the night
exercise: Why do we always see the same face of the Moon? | It spins once for each trip round the Earth | It does not spin at all as it goes round | The Earth's shadow always hides the other side | The far side always turns to face the Sun
exercise: How can a rocket speed up in empty space? | It throws gas out and the gas pushes it | It pushes off against the air behind it | The Sun's gravity alone pulls it along faster | With no friction it speeds up all by itself
exercise: A car brakes hard and you lurch forward. Which way does a helium balloon on a string lean? | Backwards | Forwards | Straight up and still | Sideways, to the left
exercise: Which of these sounds like hyperbole but is true? | The Earth carries us round the Sun at about 100,000 kilometres an hour | A cheetah can run at about 1,000 kilometres an hour | A snail can cross a whole garden in a second | A passenger plane flies at about 9,000 kilometres an hour
exercise: Why is Christmas in summer in Australia? | The southern half leans towards the Sun in December | Australia is nearer the Sun in December | Australia lies right on the equator | The Sun shines only on Australia at Christmas
test: Jack, in Sydney: "Stinkin' hot arvo, Christmas at the beach in me togs!" His pen pal in London says he's making it up. Who's right? | Jack: it is summer there at Christmas | The pen pal: Christmas is always cold | Neither: it is spring there at Christmas | The pen pal: it always snows at Christmas
test: Uncle Dave: "It's summer cos we're closest to the Sun, innit." Put him right. | The tilt makes summer; we are nearest in January | He is right, that is how the seasons work | We are only closest to the Sun in July | The Sun itself gets hotter every summer
test: "Car's doing 60, sorry, 90 kilometres an hour. How far in 20 minutes?" | 30 kilometres | 20 kilometres | 60 kilometres | 90 kilometres
test: Gran: "That car went past like a rocket, must've done a thousand miles an hour." What does she really mean? | It went very fast | It went exactly 1,000 miles an hour | It really was a rocket | It was going rather slowly
test: Mia: "The Moon's got a dark side, it's giving spooky, no cap." Put her right. | Both sides get sunlight in turn; we just never see the far one | The far side never gets any sunlight at all | The Moon has no far side, it is flat | She is right, it is always dark there
test: In a cartoon, an astronaut on the Moon looks up at a bright blue daytime sky. Fix it. | The sky would be black, with no air to scatter blue light | The sky would be green on the Moon | Nothing, the sky is blue everywhere | The sky would be orange all day long, like one long sunset
test: "Earth spins at 1,600 kilometres an hour?? Then why aren't we blown off, that's sus." What's the best answer? | The motion is smooth, and the air moves round with us | It's a lie, the Earth doesn't move at all | We are, but only a tiny bit each day | Only the people at the poles feel it
test: The tide is racing in faster than the family can walk, and they are cut off. Dad: "Just wade through, it's only a bit damp." What's wise? | Get to the highest dry ground and call the coastguard | Wade quickly through the deepest part | Swim out to sea, away from the beach | Wait on the sand until the water stops
test: Put these in order through a summer day in Britain: a short shadow pointing north, a long shadow pointing east, a long shadow pointing west. | West, then north, then east | East, then north, then west | North, then west, then east | West, then east, then north
test: "The space station does 28,000 kilometres an hour, so the astronauts must be squashed flat, bruv." Right? | No, they float, falling round the Earth with the station | Yes, they are pressed flat the whole time | Yes, so they have to lie down all day | No, because the station is really standing perfectly still in space
test: Which is the odd one out: the Moon, a phone satellite, the space station, a shooting star? | A shooting star: it is not going round the Earth | The Moon: it is not going round the Earth | A phone satellite: it is not going round the Earth | The space station: it is not going round the Earth
exam: Calculate the distance covered in a quarter of an hour by a vehicle moving uniformly at 80 kilometres per hour. | 20 kilometres | 80 kilometres | 40 kilometres | 4 kilometres
exam: Identify the month in which the Earth is nearest to the Sun. | January | July | June | December
exam: State the direction in which shadows point at sunrise. | West | East | North | South
exam: Select the correct account of the motion of a rocket beyond the atmosphere. | It expels gas, which pushes it forward | It pushes against the surrounding air | It cannot speed up at all | It is drawn along by the stars
